Z||000007||Urchin||08-27-2001||02:56 AM||||Often so called shark attacks are mere "bumps".  Sharks are obviously not intimidated by a human presence.  The fact is that most of the time (I'm not sure of the reasons) they will simply bump you.  It makes me laugh when I see all of these people who have "survived shark attacks" without a scratch on them. Do you really think if a shark was "attacking" you that you are going to walk away without injury.  I don't.  I'm not saying that shark attacks don't exist, obviously they do.  I simply think that the numbers seem exceptionally high due to these people who have perhaps embelashed the story a little.  <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/side_cut_sig_4.jpg">  <BR>If you can read this, I'm parked.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Sleeper||08-27-2001||04:40 AM||||lucky us. we have dolpins to keep the sharks away or at least i think thats why theres no shark attacks here. we have a jellyfish problem, i get stung at least once every summer. talk about pain and we don't have the clearest water either so its not like you can see them/it.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Songman||08-27-2001||08:54 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Urchin:<BR><B>Often so called shark attacks are mere "bumps".  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Most of these 'bumps' in the news lately have ended up costing someone a limb.<P>I love the ocean and always have, but I have also always had a fear of sharks. Any sane person should in my opinion. Sharks are pure predators. They live to eat. It's not about being hungry or anything like they... they just eat.<P>I lost my mind for a little while a few years ago when I was down in Pensacola. It was my last day there so we walked down to the beach and sorta just waded out knee deep in the water. All of a sudden I saw this shadow in the water ambling my way very slowly. When it went about a foot from my leg I realized that it was a shark! But he wasn't too interested in me. I, on the otherhand, was interested in him and started following him into deper water trying to get a better look. Now this was a small shark, probably about 4 feet long, and probably could not have hurt me too bad but I soon realized what I was doing and got out of the water. If you see one shark, you can usually bet others are near...<P>The surfers and anyone else can do as they wish. When there are sharks in the area, I'll be staying on the beach and giving them space.<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||7
Z||000010||JSocha||08-27-2001||10:08 AM||||Well, with the recent huricanes and their intensity, what is one to expect.  Huricanes drive the sharks into the respective areas, hense the reason there are more attackes recently due to the increased population.<P>However, and believe it or not, sharks do not like the taste of humans (so the professional researches say) and are actually afraid of humans much like we are afraid of them.  They in most cases just end up mistaking us humans on surfboards or swimming as seals, their natural and favorite food source.<P>I'm of course walrus size in nature, so I wonder if they would attack me?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif"><P>Anyways, Songman, I think the little one you were following must have been leading you out to the buffet line.  Of course, you could have been the item that was on the buffet.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Raydar||08-27-2001||10:15 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ray b:<BR><B>the place to surf there is called the shark pit<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Let me get this straight.<P>They <I>surf</I> in an area called the <I>shark pit</I>?!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>Ummm.... Nevermind. <BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Urchin||08-27-2001||12:06 PM||||    <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Songman:<BR><B> Most of these 'bumps' in the news lately have ended up costing someone a limb.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>There seems to be two main views on sharks. Those that have an image of hundreds of Great White Sharks waiting to eat people at the shoreline and those that think that sharks are harmless and want to pet them. I think a view somewhere between these two would be more realistic. In the vast majority of underwater encounters, the shark will approach a person in the water based on curiousity.  Devices emmitting electrical energy can also attract sharks.  We have also been programmed to automatically expect the worst by movies, quasi-science fiction books, media etc. The majority of incidents between humans and potentially dangerous marine life involve carelessness or aggression indulged by the human being concerned.  Put all of these points together and you hare going to undoubtedly have a certain number of cases which have been blown out of proportion.  As I said before, sure shark attacks obviously exist but I think the hype is much more prominant than the reality.<P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sharkpic_1.jpg">   <BR>"Insane risk, or calculated act of trust?"<P>I realize I will probably get flamed for saying this.  Oh well.  No harm meant.<BR>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/side_cut_sig_4.jpg">     <BR>If you can read this, I'm parked.<P>[This message has been edited by Urchin (edited 08-27-2001).]<P>[This message has been edited by Urchin (edited 08-27-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Urchin (edited 08-27-2001).]||||reg||1
