Z||000019||Ken Wittlief||08-27-2001||04:46 PM||||the press has to be up to something on this - like they have been with the supposive heat wave we had this summer [even though overall its been a relatively mild summer]<P>  look for the media spin to surface - shark attacks cause by global warming, pollution, Bush tax policies....<P> ten years ago I took my family to the ocean for a 4 day weekend - my daughter refused to go into the water past her ankles, got hysterical if we tried to force her to.<P> when we got back home found out some ditz girl at school had seen Jaws, and told her if she swims in the ocean then sharks would kill her - totally ruined her time at the beach.<P>  if I knew who that girl from school was I would have kicked her right in the butt.||||reg||1
Z||000020||artherd||08-27-2001||05:13 PM||||Think of it as Evolution in action...<P>Sharks don't really scare me, they're primative and their "brains" are barely big enough to pump blood.<P>However, I have seen an Orca get rather angry at a Great White for swiming between said Orcan and her baby...<BR>The Orca just turned, and *bit the great white in half*   <BR>...<BR>Then fed it to her baby...<P>Orcas scare me, they're intelligent mamals, and they are capable of havging *a bad day* and are unpredictable compared to the robotic nature of Sharks.<P>SCUBA divers know what's down there, and we keep going anyway... draw your own conclusions about our sanity!<P>Best!!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000021||JSocha||08-27-2001||05:25 PM||||<B>Myth:</B> Sharks are brainless eating machines. <P><B>Fact:</B> Not only are sharks' brains physically large, but young nurse sharks have been trained to come, touch and retrieve objects on command.<P>Ben, do you dive?  How long?  If I'm ever out your way, if you do, want to get together?  I haven't been able to get any dives in this year (or last).  Wife is constantly sick or working and my friend always gets put on weekend shifts when we have something planned or the weather is crummy.||||reg||1
Z||000022||starfighter007@msn.com||08-27-2001||05:42 PM||||I understand that when the surfers swim out to catch a surf they look like seals to a shark from underneath the surface||||reg||1
Z||000023||AusFiero||08-27-2001||06:21 PM||||We have a lot of great whites over here, thankfully not off the coastline of my state. Studies have shown they bump their pray to see if it is going to put up a fight. Yes...... they are not very smart and can't fathom size. An example was a diver who decided to prove a point by taking spear and went swimming with great whites. When they circled and approached he attacked them with the spear. He scared the sharks away as each had a go. Plus it has been found great whites don't really like the taste of humans anyway. They tend to take a bite, spit it out and swim away. <BR>Recently on the local news though they showed a dead whale off the SA coast. Great whites on a feeding frenzy, took them 5 days to consume the dead whale. Typically some idiot got out of a boat and was on top of the whale. They should have left him there.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.fieroshop.com.au" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
Z||000024||Sleeper||08-27-2001||09:20 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Steve Normington:<BR><B> <BR>Do you urinate on it or is that an old wives tale?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>i've heard of that but i've never done it, come-on pee of yourself? i just hold the pain as long as i can so my friends don't call me a little b*tch then go home to 3 advils and calamine lotion.||||reg||1
Z||000025||turbo86se||08-27-2001||11:11 PM||||Ausfiero, wasn't that on Discovery's Air Jaws show last week? I think it was Seal Island. Real cool show, they showed the great whites leaping out of the sea attacking a seal. <P>I've been worried myself about the shark attacks. Just yesterday I was at the beach, not too many people in the ocean. I had to jump in to cool off. It felt sooo good. The water is in the mid 80's.<P>Everytime I go fishing at the Surfside or Pawley's Pier, there's nothing but sharks around. Big ones too. Once I caught a 4' shark, and a sting ray. Got a good pic of me holding a 2' shark by it's tail. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000026||DRH||08-28-2001||12:18 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B><BR>Orcas scare me, they're intelligent mamals, and they are capable of havging *a bad day* and are unpredictable compared to the robotic nature of Sharks.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I saw a show on Orcas. One pair was out  hunting seals. The seals were on a sand bar across a small channel from a larger island. One Orca would beach himself and scare the seals across the channel where the other Orca was waiting. <P>They also showed them catch a seal alive, toss it around in the water for awhile and release it (relatively) unhurt. A very rare instance of a predator (other than man) hunting for sport and purposely releasing the catch unharmed. It shows a high degree of intelligence.<P>||||reg||1
